  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 1238 AM MDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nShowers and thunderstorms coverage continues to increase each day \nthrough Monday as the active monsoon plume remains over the CO and a \nwell defined disturbance moves through southwest flow Monday \nafternoon and night.  Thunderstorms on Sunday still look high based \nacross the southeast plains but some gradual moistening does bring \nCAPE up a bit with values approaching 1000 J/kg across southern \nareas.  Locally heavy rainfall will be the primary concern and burn \nscar flash flooding will be possible should a stronger storm impact \nthose area. Highest precipitable waters still reside out west with \nup to 150% of normal along the Continental Divide. With \nwesterly downslope flow across the southeast mountains/adjacent \nplains, confidence is low that Aspen Acres will be impacted. But\nat the same time, the risk will not be zero either. Willow burn\nscar and Chalk Cliffs stand a better chance conceptually for \nseeing excessive rainfall/flash flooding. Otherwise, \ntemperatures cool off slightly as clouds increase. Monday still \nlooks like the wettest day of the period, though timing of the \nshortwave Monday night is not well juxtaposed with peak heating,\nwhich could reduce the risk of flash flooding. Will need to \nmonitor the timing/position of a cold front as it drops through \nthe southeast plains Monday/Monday night. If the front moves in \nduring the day on Monday, east to southeast upslope flow may \nprovide a better focus and sufficient confidence in flash \nflooding for the Aspen Acres burn scar to warrant a Flash Flood \nWatch. However, not all models agree with this timing and \ndetails will need to be refined as we get closer. \n\nTuesday looks drier behind the shortwave, though there is still\nsufficient moisture for isolated afternoon and evening\nthunderstorms.  Then moisture gradually increases again as \nsouthwest flow picks up some Pacific moisture and spreads it \nacross southern CO through late week. Best precipitation chances\nappear focused across the mountains (mainly Continental Divide)\nwhere better moisture fetch will be in place. East of the \nmountains, moisture looks high based again, though at least some\nisolated thunderstorms should return to the forecast for most \nareas by Saturday. -KT",
